Set A: {(1, 2), (3, 4), (5, 6)}
  Look at the inputs (the first number of each pair): 1, 3, 5. They are all different — no input repeats. So each input has exactly one output, and Set A IS a function.

Set B: {(2, 7), (4, 9), (2, 1)}
  Look at the inputs: 2, 4, 2. The input 2 appears twice — once paired with 7 and once paired with 1. That's the same input giving two different outputs, which breaks the rule. So Set B is NOT a function.

Set C: {(1, 5), (2, 5), (3, 5)}
  Look at the inputs: 1, 2, 3 — all different. The OUTPUT 5 repeats, but that's allowed! Different inputs can share an output. Every input still has exactly one output, so Set C IS a function.

The whole test came down to one question: does any input (first number) show up more than once with different outputs? In Set B it did, so it failed. In Sets A and C every input had a single output, so they passed. Repeated outputs, as in Set C, never disqualify a relation — only repeated inputs with conflicting outputs do.
